{
  "abstract": "Aristotle (De Caelo III.8) claimed that regular tetrahedra fill space; the refutation is credited to Regiomontanus, and the history is told by Lagarias and Zong (2012). This entry proves in Lean that there is no tiling of 3-space by similar copies of the regular tetrahedron, and the same for the regular octahedron, dodecahedron and icosahedron. Tiles are convex polytopes, need not meet face to face, and may each have their own scale. With helper tiles whose dihedral angles are nonnegative rational multiples of pi (cubes, for instance), no tile is a copy of the polyhedron; in a tiling by the five Platonic polyhedra and such helpers, no tile is a dodecahedron or an icosahedron. The proof reads each dihedral angle from the set, uses that the angles round an edge add up to 2 pi (each a dihedral angle, pi or 2 pi), and that pi, arctan of the golden ratio, arccos(1/sqrt 6) and arccos(sqrt(2/3)) are linearly independent over Q, by Niven's theorem (Mathlib niven) and a field argument. Tilings are locally finite by definition, and each polyhedron is one fixed set in exact coordinates. None of these theorems is new; the entry gives their Lean proofs. Audience: researchers on tilings and polyhedra, and Mathlib contributors.",
